const v14ReadA={id:"v14-read-a",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 69-72",title:"First Rounds",description:"On her first morning as a barangay health volunteer, Joy carried a clipboard, a thermometer, and a list of twelve households. By nine she had visited two.\n\nAt the first house, an old man insisted on serving her coffee and telling her about his late wife's garden before he would let her take his blood pressure. At the second, a young mother cried while answering the questions about her baby's feeding, and Joy put the clipboard down and simply listened.\n\n'At this rate you'll finish next week,' said Aling Rosa, the senior volunteer who had come along to train her. Joy expected a scolding. Instead Rosa said, 'Write down what she told you about the feeding. Not on the form. In your notebook. The nurse will want to know.'\n\nBy late afternoon Joy had reached nine houses. Her feet ached, and the form for the last household had a coffee stain on it. Walking back, she apologized for being slow.\n\nRosa shrugged. 'The list is how we find people,' she said. 'It isn't the job.'",alt:"Short story about a new barangay health volunteer who visits fewer households than planned because she listens, and the senior volunteer who tells her the list is not the job."};
const v14ReadB={id:"v14-read-b",kind:"passage",label:"Babasahin para sa Aytem 73-76",title:"Aklatang Naglalakbay",description:"Ang mobile library ay isang lumang van na nilagyan ng mga estante, at nagdadala ito ng mga aklat sa mga barangay na malayo sa aklatang pambayan. Isang drayber at isang librarian ang kasama nito sa bawat biyahe.\n\nDumadalaw linggo-linggo ang mobile library sa apat na barangay.\n\nHalos walang humihiram sa barangay na iyon, at napag-usapan pa ng mga kawani kung dapat bang itigil na ang paghinto roon. Gayunman, nagpasya silang alamin muna ang dahilan bago gumawa ng anumang desisyon.\n\nPare-pareho ang oras ng paghinto, ngunit kakaunti ang gumagamit sa barangay na may palengke tuwing umaga. Tinanong ng mga kawani ang mga residente at nalaman nilang kasabay ng iskedyul ang oras ng pamimili at paghahatid. Inilipat nila sa hapon ang pagbisita sa barangay na iyon at nagpadala ng paalala isang araw bago dumating. Dumami ang humihiram, kaya susunod nilang susuriin kung anong uri ng aklat ang madalas hanapin upang maiayos ang laman ng sasakyan.",alt:"Teksto tungkol sa mobile library na binago ang oras sa isang barangay matapos alamin ang gawain ng residente, gumamit ng paalala, at planong suriin ang hinahanap na aklat."};
const v14ReadC={id:"v14-read-c",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 77-80",title:"The Cost of What You Give Up",description:"Economists like to say that nothing is truly free, and they mean something more precise than that everything has a price tag. Every choice uses up time, money, or effort that could have gone somewhere else. The value of the best alternative you give up is called the opportunity cost of your choice.\n\nImagine a municipal office with enough budget for one project this year: either repairing a farm-to-market road or building a new covered court. If the office chooses the court, the opportunity cost is not the court's price in pesos. It is the benefit the road would have brought, such as faster deliveries and lower transport costs for farmers.\n\nThe idea applies to individuals too. A student who spends a free afternoon working at a part-time job earns some money, but gives up the hours she could have spent studying or resting. If her grades suffer, the true cost of that afternoon may be larger than it first appeared.\n\nOpportunity cost is easy to overlook because the option you give up leaves no receipt. You can see what you bought; you rarely see what you did not get. Good decisions require looking for that invisible cost on purpose.",alt:"Economics passage defining opportunity cost with a municipal road-or-court choice and a student's part-time job, and explaining why the cost is easy to overlook."};
